1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,617
Files in the Media Library are open to anyone who knows their address.

2
00:00:05,617 --> 00:00:09,626
The Secure Media feature adds permissions for media folders.

3
00:00:09,626 --> 00:00:14,061
In this video, you enable it, open the root folder to visitors, and

4
00:00:14,061 --> 00:00:16,430
give a role access to one folder.

5
00:00:21,030 --> 00:00:25,267
Without Secure Media, every file in the Media Library is served to anyone

6
00:00:25,267 --> 00:00:26,661
who has its address.

7
00:00:26,661 --> 00:00:29,182
That suits the pictures of a public blog.

8
00:00:29,182 --> 00:00:33,150
It does not suit a contract, or a price list meant for a few people.

9
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:38,957
Secure Media adds view permissions to the media folders, so each role can

10
00:00:38,957 --> 00:00:43,629
be allowed to view all folders, the root folder, or chosen folders.

11
00:00:43,629 --> 00:00:49,070
Anyone without the permission gets 404 Not Found, as if the file did not exist.

12
00:00:53,254 --> 00:00:56,937
Here is the blog as a visitor sees it, without signing in.

13
00:00:56,937 --> 00:01:01,944
The picture behind the title is a file from the root folder of the Media Library.

14
00:01:03,542 --> 00:01:08,169
Secure Media is a feature, so it is enabled under Tools, Features.

15
00:01:08,169 --> 00:01:10,482
Let's search for Secure Media.

16
00:01:10,935 --> 00:01:12,610
Its description reads:

17
00:01:12,610 --> 00:01:16,140
Adds permissions to restrict access to media folders.

18
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:17,635
Let's click Enable.

19
00:01:18,098 --> 00:01:21,027
A message confirms that the feature was enabled.

20
00:01:21,027 --> 00:01:25,638
A warning asks you to review the role permissions, and links to the Roles page.

21
00:01:26,093 --> 00:01:29,221
Access changes as soon as the feature is enabled.

22
00:01:29,221 --> 00:01:34,168
Administrators can view every file, signed-in users can view their own media, and

23
00:01:34,168 --> 00:01:37,183
everyone else needs a permission to view files.

24
00:01:38,779 --> 00:01:43,078
Back on the blog as a visitor, the picture behind the title is gone.

25
00:01:43,078 --> 00:01:46,679
The request for the file is answered with 404 Not Found.

26
00:01:50,879 --> 00:01:53,790
Secure Media adds these view permissions:

27
00:01:53,790 --> 00:01:58,683
View media content in all folders, View media content in the root folder,

28
00:01:58,683 --> 00:02:02,399
View others media content, and View own media content.

29
00:02:03,149 --> 00:02:07,363
It also adds one permission for each folder directly under the root, such

30
00:02:07,363 --> 00:02:09,816
as View media content in folder 'press'.

31
00:02:09,816 --> 00:02:13,603
A folder permission covers that folder and the folders inside it.

32
00:02:13,603 --> 00:02:17,069
A folder you create in the root folder gets its permission.

33
00:02:17,819 --> 00:02:23,507
A folder permission also lets a role view the files stored directly in the root folder.

34
00:02:23,507 --> 00:02:30,539
Manage All Media Folders includes every view permission, and opening the Media Library requires Manage Media.

35
00:02:34,746 --> 00:02:39,443
The blog's pictures sit in the root folder, so visitors need that permission.

36
00:02:39,443 --> 00:02:43,177
Visitors who are not signed in belong to the Anonymous role.

37
00:02:43,177 --> 00:02:45,836
Let's open it from Access Control, Roles.

38
00:02:46,289 --> 00:02:50,736
Search the permissions for media content, then tick Allow for View media content

39
00:02:50,736 --> 00:02:52,029
in the root folder.

40
00:02:52,484 --> 00:02:53,668
Save the role.

41
00:02:53,668 --> 00:02:58,521
Permissions of the Anonymous role also apply to signed-in users, so the root

42
00:02:58,521 --> 00:03:00,474
folder is open to everyone.

43
00:03:02,060 --> 00:03:05,256
As a visitor, the picture behind the title is back.

44
00:03:05,256 --> 00:03:10,920
Folders other than the root stay closed, because the Anonymous role has no permission for them.

45
00:03:15,129 --> 00:03:20,653
On this site, editors prepare press material, so the Editor role should view the press folder.

46
00:03:20,653 --> 00:03:23,029
Let's open it from the list of roles.

47
00:03:23,487 --> 00:03:28,519
Searching the permissions for media content lists one permission for each folder:

48
00:03:28,519 --> 00:03:33,667
View media content in folder 'contracts', and View media content in folder 'press'.

49
00:03:34,129 --> 00:03:37,737
Tick Allow for View media content in folder 'press'.

50
00:03:37,737 --> 00:03:42,029
The contracts folder stays unticked, so editors cannot view it.

51
00:03:42,485 --> 00:03:43,618
Save the role.

52
00:03:43,618 --> 00:03:48,430
Editors can view the press folder and the folders inside it, and administrators

53
00:03:48,430 --> 00:03:50,355
keep access to every folder.

54
00:03:50,806 --> 00:03:52,224
Open the role again.

55
00:03:52,224 --> 00:03:55,497
The Effective column shows what the role ends up with:

56
00:03:55,497 --> 00:04:00,625
granted, implied by another permission, or given by the Anonymous or Authenticated role.

57
00:04:00,625 --> 00:04:04,226
The press folder is ticked, and the contracts folder is not.

58
00:04:08,426 --> 00:04:12,732
In the Media Library, a user sees only the folders that user may

59
00:04:12,732 --> 00:04:15,746
view, so a restricted folder is not listed.

60
00:04:16,496 --> 00:04:20,717
Files attached to a content item with the Attached editor of a Media

61
00:04:20,717 --> 00:04:25,566
Field follow who may view that content item, so they need no folder permission.

62
00:04:27,566 --> 00:04:32,115
Secure Media lets role permissions decide who can view each media folder, and

63
00:04:32,115 --> 00:04:34,692
answers everyone else with 404 Not Found.

64
00:04:34,692 --> 00:04:38,090
Review the permissions of your roles when you enable it.

65
00:04:38,090 --> 00:04:39,516
Thanks for watching.

